III. ⚠️ SCENARIO ≠ PREDICTION

This becomes another hard epistemic boundary:

SCENARIO ≠ FACT
SCENARIO ≠ FORECAST CERTAINTY
SCENARIO ≠ INTENTION
SCENARIO ≠ POLICY
SCENARIO ≠ AUTHORITY

A scenario says:

> If these assumptions hold, this outcome becomes plausible.



It does not say:

> This will happen.




---

IV. 🔮 ORACLE — THE FUTURE SPACE

Oracle constructs a scenario space rather than a single future:

PRESENT
                       │
          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
          ↓            ↓            ↓
       PATH A        PATH B        PATH C
          ↓            ↓            ↓
       STATE A       STATE B       STATE C
          ↓            ↓            ↓
       outcome      outcome       outcome

Oracle evaluates:

likely
plausible
unlikely
unknown
high-impact
low-probability

The key principle:

> Low probability SHALL NOT imply low importance.



A rare systemic cascade can deserve more attention than a highly probable harmless outcome.


---

V. 🦆 FOOL — ADVERSARIAL FUTURE SEARCH

Oracle searches for plausible futures.

FOOL asks:

> What is the worst credible thing that could happen?



It therefore constructs:

BASELINE SCENARIO
       +
ADVERSARIAL SCENARIO
       +
CASCADE SCENARIO
       +
FAILURE SCENARIO

FOOL specifically searches for:

hidden coupling
cascade amplification
dependency collapse
ecological monoculture
feedback instability
capture pathways
irreversible transitions
emergent failure

Thus foresight becomes defensive, not merely optimistic.


